From a3af62a7ebd6a9dae2c89abf65a890d2c9f91b79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 17:33:33 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] gweb: Fix OOB write in received_data()

received_data() splits the HTTP response headers at '\n' with memchr()
but measured each line with strlen(). A header line with a NUL octet in
it made the two disagree, so bytes_read stayed larger than the data left
in the receive buffer. The next memchr() then read past the buffer and
the '\0' written at the line end went past it too. The MMSC, or anyone
who can answer in its place on the bearer, controls this data.

Backported from ConnMan commit d1a5ede5d255 ("gweb: Fix OOB write in
received_data()") by Nathan Crandall <ncrandall@tesla.com>, which fixes
CVE-2022-32292. mmsd's gweb/ is a copy of ConnMan's from 2012 and never
got it. Upstream message:

  There is a mismatch of handling binary vs. C-string data with memchr
  and strlen, resulting in pos, count, and bytes_read to become out of
  sync and result in a heap overflow.  Instead, do not treat the buffer
  as an ASCII C-string. We calculate the count based on the return value
  of memchr, instead of strlen.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Backport [https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/network/connman/connman.git/commit/?id=d1a5ede5d255bde8ef707f8441b997563b9312bd]
---
 gweb/gweb.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/gweb/gweb.c b/gweb/gweb.c
index 4c2f95c..d434b1b 100644
--- a/gweb/gweb.c
+++ b/gweb/gweb.c
@@ -902,7 +902,7 @@ static gboolean received_data(GIOChannel *channel, GIOCondition cond,
 		}
 
 		*pos = '\0';
-		count = strlen((char *) ptr);
+		count = pos - ptr;
 		if (count > 0 && ptr[count - 1] == '\r') {
 			ptr[--count] = '\0';
 			bytes_read--;
-- 
2.53.0

